$DTYPE RXN:VAR(1):EXP_PROC
$DATUM Synthesis of Polyvinyl Pyrrolidone-Stabilized Palladium Nanoparticles.
The PVP-Pd nanoparticles were synthesized using a previously described
procedure.58,59 177.4 mg (1.0 mmol) of [PdCl4]2- (Aldrich 99%) was
dissolved in 10 mL of 0.2 M hydrochloric acid (BDH, analar) and made up
to 500 mL with water, making a 2 mM solution. The mixture was sonicated
until all of the solid had dissolved. 15 mL of the 2 mM solution was
added to a 40% ethanol/water mixture in a round-bottomed flask. 38.5 mg
(1 ímol) of poly(vinylpyrrolidone) (Sigma, molecular weight: 40 000) was
added to the flask and heated to reflux for 3 h. The spectral changes
accompanying reduction of [PdCl4]2- to Pd(0) were monitored using
UV-visible spectroscopy (see Supporting Information).50 The resulting
PVP-Pd nanoparticle solution was stored under nitrogen until use. Heck
Reaction in Solution. 74 mg (0.5 mmol) of 4-vinylbenzoic acid, 121 mg
(0.6 mmol) of 4-bromobenzoic acid, and 210 mg (2.5 mmol) of sodium
hydrogen carbonate were dissolved in 10 mL of DMF. 1 μL of a solution of
PVP-stabilized palladium nanoparticles was added,58 and the mixture was
refluxed at 100 °C for 7 h. After reaction, the solvent was removed and
the product was analyzed by mass spectrometry.
$DTYPE RXN:VAR(1):NOTES
$DATUM solid-supported catalyst, poly(vinylpyrrolidone) Sigma, molecular weight
:40000 used in second stage, Heck reaction
$DTYPE RXN:VAR(1):REFERENCE(1):TITLE
$DATUM Spatially Controlled Suzuki and Heck Catalytic Molecular Coupling
$DTYPE RXN:VAR(1):REFERENCE(1):AUTHOR
$DATUM Davis, Jason J.; Bagshaw, Claire B.; Busuttil, Katerina L.; Hanyu, Yuki;
Coleman, Karl S.
$DTYPE RXN:VAR(1):REFERENCE(1):CITATION
$DATUM Journal of the American Chemical Society (2006), 128(43), 14135-14141

